{:edb/db-before-t 1578 :edb/db-after-t 1579 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534891 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:15:31.363Z" 13194139534891 true] [17592186049979 1007 #edb/ref 17592186317806 13194139534891 true] [17592186228894 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n\n${x + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 5 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$." 13194139534891 false] [17592186228894 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/93b55a42a534e4f547b1cab1b700d96b4d5b51656f2090673eb051fefe44221d.png)" 13194139534891 true] [17592186228894 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x) = +\\infty, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x) = 3, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139534891 true] [17592186228894 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x) = +\\infty, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x) = -\\infty$\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1$, where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x) = 3, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x) = 4$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139534891 false] [17592186228896 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$\n\n$f(x)|= {1,\\begin{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is even \\\\ -1,\\end{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is odd; g(x)|= u(x - 1) + u(x - \\frac{1}{2}) + u(x - \\frac{1}{3}) + ⋯; h(x)|= ⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉$\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." 13194139534891 false] [17592186228896 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & =u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯ \\\\ h(x) & =⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." 13194139534891 true] [17592186228896 1145 "Suppose $I\\subseteq \\mathbb R$. A function $f: I \\to \\mathbb R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\\subset I$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[MATH: [a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)]$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6669/Source/Images/d382ac4af0a40eb467736e4be9da14d2.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1]$. Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\dfrac12,\\dfrac13,\\dfrac14,\\ldots$, so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2,4,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[MATH: [a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)]$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6669/Source/Images/a5b2b21c0b5d491bbb537537aec2e88e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139534891 false] [17592186228896 1145 "Suppose $I\\subseteq \\mathbb R.$ A function $f: I \\to \\mathbb R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\\subset I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,\\ldots,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/3ce7aa18de332c3eb1bc57b6bf7c50acf1573717d642c731a18a85f4f9fb813a.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\dfrac12,\\dfrac13,\\dfrac14,\\ldots,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2,4,\\ldots,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/9a5cc55dc36927437d16d09b4e491305efebac9d9a8995a741d62b6395a101bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139534891 true] [17592186229128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317813 13194139534891 true] [17592186229128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317820 13194139534891 true] [17592186229128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317827 13194139534891 true] [17592186229128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317834 13194139534891 true] [17592186229129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317841 13194139534891 true] [17592186229129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317848 13194139534891 true] [17592186229129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317855 13194139534891 true] [17592186229129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317862 13194139534891 true] [17592186229129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186317869 13194139534891 true] [17592186229686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186228579 13194139534891 false] [17592186229686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317807 13194139534891 true] [17592186229686 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534891 true] [17592186229686 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x ∈ [- 1, 1]$.\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}, g(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0,h(x) = \\cos x$." 13194139534891 false] [17592186229686 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139534891 true] [17592186229686 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=0$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ except at the point $x=0,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317805 1089 "e-40653" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317805 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/1146bea1d20a4478e32e2d6c1000dc43dc14ae8a592660fc7618792c8231c671.png)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/c07d756afce881fc79fc5da71aa8fb12a913a6ae244971b6519b9cd6b3c1a9a9.png)" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317805 1113 #uuid "2444336d-4e2f-5fae-8ec6-dd08736162f1" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317805 1145 "A function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/12a2b198bc9d42170a9aeb3d0390ccee0463bab7c637bf4a4642750332aeeba0.png) is **piecewise smooth** on ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/ba299c06d373f7efc4870693613b428130d4aaa58dc9b4736a10934bd5