{h}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,$ $g,$ and $h.$\"" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317892 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317893 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317894 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317895 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317896 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1041 "selection" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317894 13194139534891 true] [17592186317891 1110 #uuid "0d34aefc-80d4-5958-9e37-1509eb65ee07" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317892 1034 "$f$ and $g$ only" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317892 1111 #uuid "15ff1af1-f654-58d6-8147-a0a9261e9aff" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317893 1034 "$g$ and $h$ only" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317893 1111 #uuid "c9f6b3ec-0960-5e35-b339-8a0645e568c5" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317894 1034 "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317894 1111 #uuid "88e67d64-9813-5f96-9d36-1cd7751de885" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317895 1034 "$f$ only" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317895 1111 #uuid "8b34b6f6-66e9-5c76-95b8-088ce7f730dd" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317896 1034 "$f$ and $h$ only" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317896 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317896 1111 #uuid "c3886322-aff1-5f43-a44b-f41e1789a29f" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317897 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317898 13194139534891 true] [17592186317897 1089 "q-394142" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317897 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534891 true] [17592186317897 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})\n$$" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317897 1113 #uuid "1a804ed1-fbfc-5aeb-84ec-d84d0298664d" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317897 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{3/7}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on each open interval, but the limit of ${g}^{′}(x)$ as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite because $\\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}$ becomes unbounded. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2/3}),\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. Because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})$ approaches $1$ while $\\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}$ becomes unbounded near $x=0,$ the derivative gets infinitely large. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions.\"" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317899 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317900 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317901 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317902 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317903 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1041 "selection" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317903 13194139534891 true] [17592186317898 1110 #uuid "fd058a2f-f310-5240-864e-e47a3f8ffc95" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317899 1034 "$f$ and $g$ only" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317899 1111 #uuid "055ba5ce-5641-5a7a-b830-f641bb8b3257" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317900 1034 "$h$ only" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317900 1111 #uuid "d7161898-b045-5482-b116-296d60561ff4" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317901 1034 "$g$ only" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534891 true] [17592186317901 1111 #uuid "86fb389f-7d13-5681-a331-7a9c1e324b9e" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317902 1034 "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" 13194139534891 true] [17592186317902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 1